kboard).", "tags": ["countable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(countable) Tailor's chalk.", "tags": ["countable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable, climbing, gymnastics) A white powdery substance used to prevent hands slipping from holds when climbing, or losing grip in weight-lifting or gymnastics, sometimes but not always limestone-chalk, often magnesium carbonate (MgCO₃).", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": ["when working out your next move, it's a good idea to get some more chalk from the bag"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, military, countable) A platoon-sized group of airborne soldiers.", "tags": ["US", "countable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, sports, chiefly basketball, horse racing) The favorite in a sporting event.", "tags": ["US", "countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, sports, chiefly basketball) The prediction that there will be no upsets, and the favored competitor will win.", "tags": ["US", "countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "OK, let's get rid of the chalk players right away. The chalk likes North Carolina. Dean Smith has taken Carolina to the Final Four six times.", "reference": "1982 March 22, Phil Musick, “And the pick here is - Georgetown over Houston”, in Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, page 13:"}, {"text": "Excuse us for sticking with the chalk, but the predicted winners are Afternoon Deelites in the Derby, Oliver McCall over Larry Holmes, Nick Faldo in the Masters, and Al Unser Jr. in the Grand Prix.", "reference": "1995 April 6, “Notes on a Scorecard”, in Los Angeles Times, archived from the original on 06 Mar 2012, retrieved 19 Mar 2009, page C3:"}, {"text": "Instead, he played the chalk and selected the No. 1 overall seed in the tournament.", "reference": "2008 March 24, Jason Bauman, “Non-news of the week: Obama picks North Carolina”, in Beacon-News, Aurora, Illinois, archived from the original on 03 Mar 2012, retrieved 19 Mar 2009:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃɔːk/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃoːk/", "tags": ["British", "General Australian", "New Zealand", "Southern", "Standard"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃɔk/", "tags": ["General American", "Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃɑk/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃɒk/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃolk/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/t͡ʃalk/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "chalks", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "chalking", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "chalked", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "chalked", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "chaulk", "tags": ["alternative", "dated"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To apply chalk to anything, such as the tip of a billiard cue.", "tags": [], "examples": ["chalk your hands before climbing"], "quotes": [{"text": "After a leg stretch, we set off again at 11:30 hours in charge of U.S.A. No. 1736 (Miss Ohio had been chalked on her), a 2-8-0 utility engine.", "reference": "1944 January and February, Major J. C. F. Lloyd Williamson, “Ambulance Trains in Algeria and Tunisia”, in Railway Magazine, page 6:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To record something, as on a blackboard, using chalk.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To use powdered chalk to mark the lines on a playing field.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(figurative) To record a score or event, as if on a chalkboard.", "tags": ["figuratively"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "This Book it chaulketh out before thine eyes / The man that ſeeks the everlaſting Prize: / It ſhevvs you vvhence he comes, vvhither he goes, / VVhat he leaves undone; alſo vvhat he does: / It alſo ſhovvs you hovv he runs, and runs, / Till he unto the Gate of Glory comes.", "reference": "1678, John Bunyan, “The Author’s Apology for His Book”, in The Pilgrim’s Progress from This World, to That which is to Come: […], London: […] Nath[aniel] Ponder […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To manure (land) with chalk.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Land that is chalked", "reference": "1707, J[ohn] Mortimer, The Whole Art of Husbandry; or, The Way of Managing and Improving of Land. […], London: […] J[ohn] H[umphreys] for H[enry] Mortlock […], and J[onathan] Robinson […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To make white, as if with chalk; to make pale; to bleach.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Fear Stared in her eyes, and chalked her face", "reference": "1847, Alfred Tennyson, “(please specify the page number, or |part=Prologue, I to VII, or conclusion)”, in The Princess: A Medley, London: Edward Moxon, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalk", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}